<title>USA: Dry weather continues to stress Illinois crops.</title>
<headline>Dry weather continues to stress Illinois crops.</headline>
<dateline>SPRINGFIELD, Ill. 1997-08-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>The following is the weekly crop summary from the Illinois Agricultural Statistics Service for the week ending August 3.</p>
<p>Dry conditions continue to adversely affect nearly every acre of crops across the state last week with cooler temperatures moderating the impact only slightly.  A few counties saw acreage in the Conservation Reserve Program released for emergency grazing and haying with more expecting release this week as pastures dry up and farmers are forced to feed a short hay crop to their livestock.  Cooler temperatures eased the stress on livestock but farmers were hauling water as wells began to go dry.  Crops on timber and sandy soils were suffering the most with reports of stunted soybean plants and corn firing (drying) up towards the  ear.  Peach and oat harvest continued with good results for both crops being reported.  Other farm activities last week included baling hay and straw, scouting and spraying for spider mites and leafhoppers and attending county fairs.</p>
